WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.760
Authorization failures happen when an application does not consistently verify whether a

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:09.540
signed-in user can perform a specific action or access a specific record.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:14.720
Authentication proves identity, but authorization enforces what that identity may do.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.660
Privilege escalation can let a standard user perform administrative actions or view data

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:23.000
belonging to another customer, department, or tenant.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:27.860
In SaaS systems, this can quickly become a customer trust and SOC 2 concern.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:33.840
Insecure direct object references occur when identifiers such as account IDs, invoice IDs,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.340
file names,

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:40.280
or project IDs are exposed without checking ownership or permission for the target object.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:45.620
Access control bypasses often appear in APIs, hidden routes, forced browsing,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:50.560
disabled client-side controls, or role values supplied by the browser.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.060
Sensitive authorization decisions must be enforced on the server.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.880
Apply least privilege to users, services, and administrators.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:02.020
Enforce server-side authorization on every sensitive action and object.

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.320
Use role-based access control with clear ownership and review processes.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.940
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC6.1 - Logical access controls;

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:19.400
CC6.2 - Authentication controls; CC6.3 - Authorization management.
